# Synthetic operator callback qualification

These development tools exercise the actual original registered `fixOperatorExpression` callback and public Rust F12 APIs on 42 named synthetic inputs. They do not parse expression text, build/link a model, implement a native fallback or accept a user feature. Compiler/native/browser/Git execution belongs to the coordinator. This author prepared source only.

## Matched setup and recorded phases

The immutable corpus declares each descriptor and target path. Inputs with an explicit absent `operands` field use bare native synthetic fields and bare public Rust allocation. Other input nodes use actual native `AstReflection.createNode` and actual Rust `Builder.allocate` plus `defaults`; setup calls must match. This tests absence without inventing a Rust field-removal API. Native own `undefined` is retained as a distinct value. Rust has no corresponding `Value` variant, so two cases record explicit input restrictions rather than substituting absence.

Native operands are attached through actual `AstReflection.assignNode`, including containers/cache side effects. Rust assignment preserves the same structural inputs; public immutable parenting is established at finish. The original callback is obtained from the real `SysMLCstNodeBuilder.postprocessingMap` and invoked with real empty-filesystem SysML services/reflection. No replacement reflection, factory, callback or guessed function result is used. Other registered callbacks are observed and remain uninvoked.

Every input state and each raw before/after-handler state is retained. A separate, labelled synthetic native completion calls the original `assignMandatoryProperties` on structural nodes; Rust `Builder.finish` performs its actual defaulting and parenting. Raw post-handler and completed states remain distinct. This completion is a harness/kernel comparison phase, not an asserted native parser phase. Restricted Rust cases publish no arena; no completion is inferred for them.

Native graphs retain every own key, descriptor, value, array order, stable object ID and alias. Accessors are inventoried without evaluation. The Rust observer captures every public field of tracked input/returned nodes before finish and every final Arena node. Private builder/orphan state, JS array identity/descriptors, immediate parenting and cache lifecycle remain explicit qualification limits.

## Commands and bindings

The coordinator creates fresh owned `/workspace` outputs and binds exact source, dependency, manifest/lock, tool, runtime, corpus, executable and result bytes in its receipt:

```text
node operator_oracle.cjs ABS_PINNED_BUNDLE ABS_CORPUS ABS_NEW_NATIVE_JSON
# Coordinator-owned Cargo manifest: serde_json plus actual syson-ast and syson-ast-adapter path dependencies.
cargo run --release --offline --locked --manifest-path ABS_MANIFEST --bin operator-dump -- ABS_CORPUS
python3 compare_operator.py --corpus ABS_CORPUS --native ABS_NATIVE_JSON --rust ABS_RUST_JSON --out ABS_NEW_REPORT --self-test
```

The original oracle checks the complete pinned bundle, unique pre-CLI boundary and four original module hashes. It uses current Node/V8 and real builtin/process objects; the VM is not a security sandbox. Canonical input paths and new output parents must remain under `/workspace`; exclusive output creation preserves existing files and symlink leaves. Rust emits stdout; the coordinator owns its fresh capture. All 42 ordered IDs and the exact corpus SHA remain required. A new corpus requires reviewed source changes.

The comparator strictly validates public types/IDs, own-property inventories and descriptor flags. It compares all structural fields and cache values, node/array order, aliases, exact number bits, UTF-16 strings, containers and absent/null distinctions at input, each handler step, raw post-handler and completion phases. It retains all differences; cache fields are not dropped. There is no blanket OPEN disposition for noncache field mismatches. Metadata unavailable through the public Rust API remains specifically OPEN.

Native void return is checked as actual `undefined`; it is never converted to a Rust `Report`. Rust reports are retained and validated against actual public nodes. Four truthy nonarray cases preserve typed `UnsupportedOperands` and unchanged Rust state as an explicit admission-policy difference. Thirteen complete-pipeline divergence controls cover case loss/order, Boolean handles, missing container, unexpected origin, absent/null, primitive type, signed zero, operand order, alias cycles, fabricated native returns, other-handler invocation and corpus binding. Controls must change the actual comparison or fail its input contract; an unchanged baseline cannot pass them.

The report intentionally returns exit 1 while required differences or OPEN lifecycle, runtime and caller evidence remain. Normal and `python3 -O` results must preserve the same guards. Rust quota/cancellation/foreign-owner/spent-ID behavior, full cache/P14 closure, configured native Node20/deployed artifact correspondence, real parser/importer consumers, UI and performance remain separate gates. Hashes and passing component tests alone establish none of those claims.
